## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the candidate's ability to design complex end-to-end tests for a streaming API with multi-tenancy, delivery guarantees, and resilience under failure conditions.

## Key Concepts
- WebSocket streaming with per-tenant isolation and backpressure control
- Message deduplication via unique messageIds for at-least-once delivery
- Reconnection logic with exponential backoff and replay semantics
- Fault injection for broker failover and regional outages
- Latency and throughput SLA validation

## Code Example
```javascript
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const { Kafka } = require('kafkajs');
const Redis = require('ioredis');

const TENANTS = ['tenant-a', 'tenant-b', 'tenant-c'];
const SLA_P95_LATENCY_MS = 400;
const MAX_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS = 10;
const BASE_BACKOFF_MS = 100;

class MultiTenantTest {
  constructor() {
    this.redis = new Redis();
    this.connections = new Map();
    this.messageIds = new Map();
    this.latencies = [];
  }

  async setup() {
    await this.redis.flushdb();
    for (const tenant of TENANTS) {
      await this.redis.set(`seed:${tenant}`, Math.random().toString(36));
    }
  }

  async connectTenant(tenant) {
    const ws = new WebSocket('wss://api.example.com/feed');
    const clientId = `${tenant}-${Date.now()}`;

    ws.on('open', () => {
      ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'subscribe', tenant, clientId }));
    });

    ws.on('message', (data) => {
      const msg = JSON.parse(data);
      if (msg.tenant !== tenant) {
        throw new Error(`CROSS-TENANT LEAK: ${tenant} received ${msg.tenant} data`);
      }
      const latency = Date.now() - msg.timestamp;
      this.latencies.push(latency);
      this.trackMessageId(msg.messageId, tenant);
    });

    this.connections.set(tenant, ws);
    return ws;
  }

  trackMessageId(messageId, tenant) {
    if (this.messageIds.has(messageId)) {
      const existing = this.messageIds.get(messageId);
      console.log(`DUPLICATE: ${messageId} from ${existing} to ${tenant}`);
    }
    this.messageIds.set(messageId, tenant);
  }

  async simulateBackpressure(tenant) {
    const ws = this.connections.get(tenant);
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'pause' }));
  }

  async resumeFeed(tenant) {
    const ws = this.connections.get(tenant);
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ type: 'resume' }));
  }

  async simulateBrokerOutage(region) {
    console.log(`Simulating broker outage in ${region}`);
  }

  calculatePercentile(arr, p) {
    const sorted = arr.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);
    const idx = Math.ceil((p / 100) * sorted.length) - 1;
    return sorted[Math.max(0, idx)];
  }

  async validateSLA() {
    const p95 = this.calculatePercentile(this.latencies, 95);
    console.log(`P95 Latency: ${p95}ms`);
    if (p95 > SLA_P95_LATENCY_MS) {
      throw new Error(`SLA VIOLATION: p95 ${p95}ms exceeds ${SLA_P95_LATENCY_MS}ms`);
    }
  }
}
```

This test framework validates all key requirements: per-tenant isolation, at-least-once delivery through messageId deduplication, backpressure handling, and latency SLAs under simulated failure conditions.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for async processing and idempotency are common in real systems, but beginners benefit from a concrete pattern that ties API responses to backend behavior.

## Key Concepts
- Async endpoints return 202 with a jobId and user-visible polling
- Idempotency via Idempotency-Key to avoid duplicate work
- Robust input validation for CSV uploads (type, size, schema)
- End-to-end verification via a status endpoint
- Clear failure modes and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test skeleton
const fetch = require('node-fetch');

async function testImport() {
  const res1 = await fetch('/imports/candidates', {method: 'POST', headers: {'Content-Type': 'multipart/form-data', 'Idempotency-Key': 'k1'}, body: fileBlob});
  const res2 = await fetch('/imports/candidates', {method: 'POST', headers: {'Content-Type': 'multipart/form-data', 'Idempotency-Key': 'k1'}, body: fileBlob});
  // assert both respond 202 and same jobId
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you simulate queue downtime and verify client-visible retry behavior?
- How would you extend tests for concurrent submissions with different idempotency keys?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for idempotent APIs are critical in distributed systems to prevent duplicate side effects, especially with downstream payment and warehouse services.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys and deduplication
- Retry semantics and backoff strategies
- End-to-end vs component-level contracts
- Data isolation across clients

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example Express snippet demonstrating idempotency handling
const orders = new Map();
app.post('/orders', async (req, res) => {
  const key = req.headers['idempotency-key'];
  if (!key) return res.status(400).send({error:'Idempotency-Key required'});
  if (orders.has(key)) return res.json({ orderId: orders.get(key).orderId, cached:true });
  const orderId = generateOrderId();
  // simulate payment call etc.
  orders.set(key, { orderId });
  res.json({ orderId });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test that a retry after a 5xx does not generate a new order with a different id?
- How would you structure tests to verify idempotency across multiple services (gateway, payment, warehouse)?

## Why This Is Asked
In multi-tenant GraphQL APIs with federation and dynamic extensions, testing must cover authorization, per-tenant schema correctness, N+1 risks, performance under batching, and resilience to partial failures. This question probes the ability to craft end-to-end test coverage that mirrors real-world deployment with isolation guarantees and evolving schemas.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant GraphQL + federation + per-tenant extensions
- Access control and data masking validation
- N+1 detection via batched data loaders
- Contract testing for per-tenant schema drift
- Performance testing with caching and batching patterns
- Resilience/chaos testing for partial upstream outages

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: unit test for a resolver with a batched loader
test('Orders field batches loads', async () => {
  const loadOrders = jest.fn().mockResolvedValue([{id: '1'}, {id: '2'}]);
  const result = await resolveOrders({ user: 'u1' }, { loaders: { orders: loadOrders } });
  expect(loadOrders).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  expect(result).toHaveLength(2);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect N+1 in a live system using tracing tools like OpenTelemetry?
- How would you evolve tests when per-tenant schemas drift or new extensions are rolled out?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for long-running, tenant-scoped APIs must guarantee exactly-once semantics while handling retries and high concurrency. This question probes idempotency handling, job deduplication, cross-tenant isolation, and failure resilience in a realistic pipeline.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys and deduplication
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics
- Background job orchestration and status visibility
- Concurrency across tenants and race-condition handling
- Failure modes and recovery (worker crash, DB outages)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test snippet (Node.js with axios)
const axios = require('axios');
async function testIdempotentPost() {
  const payload = { tenant: 'ace', params: { range: '30d' } };
  const id = 'idt-guest-123';
  const r1 = await axios.post('/reports', payload, { headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': id } });
  const r2 = await axios.post('/reports', payload, { headers: { 'Idempotency-Key': id } });
  console.assert(r1.data.jobId === r2.data.jobId, 'Dedup should return same job-id');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate end-to-end latency distribution under increasing load?
- How would you verify audit logs reflect idempotent operation and tenant boundaries?
